  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Showers return tonight, then breezy conditions \nThursday.   \n\nA deep trough and closed low will move into the Great Lakes region \nthrough Thursday. Surface low pressure will move from the Central \nGreat Lakes to southern Quebec while a cold front moves across the \nregion. A deep southerly flow originating from the Gulf and southern \nAtlantic Ocean will send a plume of moisture into the eastern Great \nLakes region. Forcing from the surface to 500mb and deep moisture \nwill support high confidence for showers across the region. This \nwill provide some relief to the recent dry spell, however the \nresidence time of showers will be short-lived as the plume of \nmoisture moves east of the region by Thursday afternoon. Forecast \nrainfall amounts are 0.1- 0.25\" across western NY, even less across \nthe Genesee Valley and Finger Lakes region, and 0.25-0.50\" across \nnorth-central NY with the highest amounts expected across the Tug \nHill region. \n\nMostly dry weather is expected Thursday behind the front. A tight \npressure gradient and 30-40kt low-level jet will support gusty winds \nacross the region. The strongest winds will be northeast of Lake \nErie with gusts up to 35 mph are expected. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Unsettled weather and cooler temperatures Friday \nthrough the weekend.\n\nThe upper level trough and closed low will move overhead Friday \nthrough Saturday. A cold front is expected to move across the region \nand temperatures will continue to trend downward through the period. \nModest moisture and cooler weather will support showery activity \nespecially during the daytime hours. Cooler air will wrap around the \nclosed low Friday night through Saturday night and lake enhanced \nshowers are expected downwind of the lakes. Confidence in the \nprobability of precipitation forecast decreases Sunday into the \nfollowing week as models diverge in the evolution of the mid-level \ntrough.",
